      "writtenOn": "2026-08-27T17:36:19Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Given the comment above related to `HOOK_SYSJUMP`, `watchdog_reload` uses a `K_SPINLOCK`, is this safe? or should we move this before `interrupt_disable_all` to ensure proper watchdog reset?",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 666,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 668,
        "endChar": 18
      },
      "revId": "d88166f85239aff853f1e079fbdbd98d60ee7b5c",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "f77b0d7e_da810995",
        "filename": "common/system.c",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 668,
      "author": {
        "id": 1390635
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-27T23:39:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "K_SPINLOCK works as expected even with interrupts disabled, technically a no-op since interrupts are already disabled. \n\nwatchdog_reload is specifically intended to be ISR safe, which means the constraints are similar to the constraints for HOOK_SYSJUMP handlers (no sleep, yield, interrupt-driven drivers, mutexes, or deferred functions).\n\nPrefer to keep this close to the jump to maximize the watchdog headroom for the next image.\n\nLegacy EC had similar functionality, specifically `DECLARE_HOOK(HOOK_SYSJUMP, watchdog_reload, HOOK_PRIO_LAST)`.",
      "parentUuid": "32451163_1d1b5a1f",
      "range": {
